# Azure Load Balancer + NSG Flow Explained (Hinglish)

# Scenario

Environment:

* Public DNS:

  * `erp.******.in`
* Public IP attached to Azure Load Balancer:

  * `4.***.***.***`
* Backend VM:

  * `10.0.*.*`
* Load Balancer:

  * Standard SKU
* Protocol:

  * HTTPS (443)

---

# Actual Traffic Flow

```text
Internet User
   ↓
erp.spinfra.in
   ↓
Public IP
4.***.***.***
   ↓
Azure Load Balancer
   ↓
Backend VM
10.0.*.*:443
```

---

# Biggest Confusion

Question:

```text
Agar sara traffic Public IP → Load Balancer par aa raha hai,
then NSG me:

1. AzureLoadBalancer allow kyu?
2. Any/Internet allow kyu?
```

---

# Important Concept

Azure Load Balancer me 2 alag types ka traffic hota hai:

| Traffic Type         | Source in NSG     |
| -------------------- | ----------------- |
| Real User Traffic    | Internet / Any    |
| Health Probe Traffic | AzureLoadBalancer |

---

# 1. Real User Traffic

Example:

User browser me open karta hai:

```text
https://erp.spinfra.in
```

Flow:

```text
Laptop
   ↓
Internet
   ↓
Azure LB Public IP
   ↓
Backend VM
```

NSG ko source dikhega:

```text
Internet
```

NOT:

```text
AzureLoadBalancer
```

Isliye ye rule mandatory hai:

| Priority | Source | Port | Action |
| -------- | ------ | ---- | ------ |
| 130      | Any    | 443  | Allow  |

Example:

```text
allow-https-any
```

---

# 2. Health Probe Traffic

Azure Load Balancer continuously check karta hai:

```text
"Backend VM healthy hai ya nahi?"
```

Ye traffic Azure infrastructure bhejta hai.

Flow:

```text
Azure Load Balancer Probe
   ↓
Backend VM:443
```

NSG source dikhega:

```text
AzureLoadBalancer
```

Isliye ye rule bhi mandatory hai:

| Priority | Source            | Port | Action |
| -------- | ----------------- | ---- | ------ |
| 120      | AzureLoadBalancer | 443  | Allow  |

Example:

```text
allow-https-from-loadbalancer
```

---

# What Happens If Only AzureLoadBalancer Rule Exists?

## Result

```text
Health probe works
Backend healthy dikhega
```

BUT:

```text
Real users blocked ho jayenge
```

Browser error:

```text
ERR_CONNECTION_TIMED_OUT
```

Exactly ye production issue tha.

---

# What Happens If Only Any Rule Exists?

## Result

Public users may work.

BUT:

```text
Health probe fail ho sakta hai
Backend unhealthy ho sakta hai
```

Especially in Standard SKU Load Balancer.

---

# Correct Production Architecture

Both rules required:

| Rule                    | Purpose             |
| ----------------------- | ------------------- |
| AzureLoadBalancer → 443 | Health Probe        |
| Any → 443               | Public User Traffic |

---

# Recommended NSG Rules

| Priority | Rule Name                     | Source            | Port | Purpose             |
| -------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------- | ---- | ------------------- |
| 100      | allow-ssh-admin               | 10.0.*.*/24       | 22   | SSH from Jump VM    |
| 120      | allow-https-from-loadbalancer | AzureLoadBalancer | 443  | LB Health Probe     |
| 130      | allow-https-any               | Any               | 443  | Public HTTPS Access |
| 4000     | deny-ssh-internet             | Any               | 22   | Block Public SSH    |

---

# Why Azure Standard Load Balancer Confuses People

Because Standard SKU Load Balancer is:

```text
Secure by Default
```

Meaning:

Even if:

* Public IP exists
* Load Balancer exists
* Backend healthy exists

Traffic still blocked rahega unless NSG explicitly allow kare.

---

# Important Learning

## AzureLoadBalancer Source Means

```text
Azure infrastructure internal traffic
```

NOT public internet users.

---

# Easy Real-Life Analogy

Suppose:

```text
Load Balancer = Hotel Reception
Backend VM = Hotel Room
```

## Public User Traffic

Guest enters hotel:

```text
Guest
   ↓
Reception
   ↓
Room
```

Need:

```text
Public Entry Allowed
```

---

## Health Probe Traffic

Hotel management checks:

```text
"Room operational hai ya nahi?"
```

This is internal staff traffic.

Need separate permission.

---

# Final Production Flow

```text
Internet User
   ↓
Public IP
   ↓
Azure Load Balancer
   ↓
Health Probe Checks Backend
   ↓
Backend VM
```

NSG allows:

```text
1. AzureLoadBalancer → 443
2. Any → 443
```

Both are required.

---

# Final Key Takeaway

| Rule                    | Needed? | Why                 |
| ----------------------- | ------- | ------------------- |
| AzureLoadBalancer → 443 | YES     | Health probe        |
| Any → 443               | YES     | Real internet users |
| SSH from Internet       | NO      | Security risk       |

---

# Best Practice

Always:

* Allow only required ports publicly
* Keep SSH/RDP private
* Use Jump VM/Bastion for administration
* Use Standard SKU Load Balancer
* Use NSGs as primary security layer

This is enterprise-grade Azure production architecture.


